"""
cube_push_perc.py
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Reactive push controller — architecture faithful to the Methodology chapter
of the thesis (Heins et al. 2023 + lateral centering), generalized to
convex primitives (cube/rectangle, cylinder).

This version consolidates every fix validated during debugging sessions:
  - Initial object position read from Drake's default context (always
    reflects the SDF's actual <pose>, regardless of what the caller passes
    as an argument).
  - Correct sign convention for contact_force() (Drake returns the force
    on body B of the pair; the right inversion is needed depending on
    which body — hand or object — plays the role of A or B).
  - Wrist link collision: all 4 wrist links (panda_link5-8) are excluded
    from colliding with the object. A narrower exclusion (panda_link8
    only, to let the forearm visually collide with the object for
    realism) was tried and reverted: _measure_force() only measures/
    corrects contact forces between the object and panda_hand/panda_link8
    — if link5/6/7 are allowed to touch the object, Drake integrates that
    force into the real physics (the object moves) but the controller
    never sees it and can't correct for it. This produced unpredictable,
    position-dependent divergence on the cylinder. See the constant's
    comment below for the exact empirical signature and how to safely
    re-narrow the exclusion for a one-off visual demo.
  - Forward speed profile that decelerates progressively before the
    target (wide braking zone + floor speed), decoupled from the lateral
    centering term, which stays at full strength at all times.
  - Targeted damping of the object's own EXCESS velocity (never its
    legitimate velocity), computed at the correct time step (1 kHz, in
    _update(), not in the 1 Hz log).
  - Widened tolerance margin on the fallback mechanisms (prolonged lost
    contact / stall) to accept a stop close to the target without
    crossing the strict success threshold.
  - Cube/rectangle: the active face axis is FROZEN at planning time
    (_plan_approach / _plan_reposition), and only the object's OWN
    rotation (theta_o, if the cube genuinely rotates) turns it afterward
    — it is never re-chosen from d_hat on every tick anymore. Bug fixed:
    re-choosing from d_hat exposes the controller to a face flip if the
    object overshoots the target (d_hat reverses), with no actual object
    rotation to justify it — this was the cause of the instability
    observed at the end of a push (a jump of several cm in a single time
    step, followed by a stall / failed reposition).
  - Rectangle: independent half_extent_x / half_extent_y (instead of a
    single half_extent assuming a square cube). The center→face distance
    used for planning, geometric force measurement, and contact detection
    is now computed via _current_face_offset(), which picks the right
    half-side depending on the active face's axis — cylinder unaffected
    (always half_extent = radius).
  - Directional low-pass filter (D_HAT_BETA) on d_hat, to prevent the
    "flick" observed empirically near the target (d_hat can rotate by
    more than 140° in ~1s if the object slightly overshoots — this used
    to flip the cylinder's push direction and K_LAT's lateral correction
    almost instantly, producing a sudden jump and a force reading pinned
    at the geometric fallback's ceiling). K_LAT itself is not attenuated
    by this filter — only the direction it acts on is stabilized.
  - DONE_THRESHOLD set to 25mm — physically justified (see the constant's
    comment): stays above WAYPOINT_THRESHOLD (the Cartesian position
    controller's own precision floor elsewhere in the FSM) and stays
    small relative to the tested objects (~25% of the cylinder's radius,
    ~33% of the cube's half-side).
  - final_dist_mm is recomputed from the actual final simulation state in
    run_trial(), not trusted from the controller's internal cache — the
    cached value can go stale if a trial ends in REPOSITION/APPROACH, or
    even in DONE if the active retract phase keeps moving the object
    while contact force stays pinned at the fallback estimator's ceiling.

ARCHITECTURE (see thesis §4.6-4.7)
──────────────────────────────────────
  Objective 1 — Where to push on the object's geometry:
      → get_approach_direction() chooses the face/approach direction
        (cube: nearest face normal, accounting for yaw; cylinder:
        continuous radial direction).
      → The lateral centering term (§4.7.2) keeps the contact point
        centered on that face throughout the push.

  Objective 2 — How to command the end-effector to reach that velocity:
      → v_cmd (Cartesian) → impedance → F_trans = Kv(v_cmd - v_sph)
      → τ = Jv^T F_trans - g(q), saturated at the Franka's motor limits.

  Objective 3 — Regulate the command and confirm contact:
      → The push direction is corrected by force feedback (Heins' law,
        §4.7.1).
      → Admittance saturation + the braking profile regulate the command
        without a separate PI loop.
      → Contact is confirmed by Drake (primary source) or geometric
        penetration (fallback) — always on the object's REAL position,
        never the perceived one (a force sensor is not a camera).
"""

